#
# prima-materia - the Zoroastrian transmission
#
# Order of the file: the tradition, then the sending of a soul under mang and
# what the transmission demands around it, then what the traveller is said to
# meet, then the two purifications and the caution that hangs on one of them.
# The instance was registered in traditions/registry-west-asia.ttl and is
# moved here now that passages have been read.
#
# The file is opened for the reason SPEC section 14 gives priority to: this
# transmission states procedures with numbers, deadlines and, in one case, a
# consequence of doing it wrong that is stated for the world and not only for
# the practitioner.
#

pmt:Zoroastrian a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Zoroastrian transmission"@en, "Zoroastrische Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Iranian transmission of a dualist cosmology, of a soul journey undertaken under a named preparation and reported on return, of an accounting at a bridge whose width answers to the traveller, and of purifications stated in numbers of holes, washings and nights."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Arda Wiraz Namag, chapters 1 to 5, in the Haug and West translation; Hadhoxt Nask, fragments 2 and 3, in Darmesteter's translation; Vendidad, fargards 8 and 9, in Darmesteter's translation. Not opened: Yasht 19 on the glory, Menog i Xrad, Dadestan i Denig, the Denkard, and the Kirdir inscriptions. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#ERNTE."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route stays at disputed and is not moved, but the reason is now sharper than at registration. The earliest dated Iranian witness of an otherworld journey is not literary but epigraphic, in the inscriptions of the mage Kirdir under Wahram the Second, that is 274 to 293; the Book of the Watchers is third or second century before the era. Every Pahlavi book read here reached its surviving shape in the ninth century or later. Whoever wants to read the Iranian material as the source of the Jewish and Christian ascent literature must argue from the Avestan passages and not from these books. See prima-materia#KONTAKT."@en ;
    skos:note "Two editions stand behind the wording used here and they are of different weight. Haug and West of 1872 rests on a single, partly Pazand-contaminated witness; Gignoux 1984 and Vahman 1986 are the critical editions and were not reachable in full. Where a number in this file matters, it was read in the Haug and West text and the note on the node says so."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Avesta (Gathas, Yasna, Vendidad, Yashts); Hadhoxt Nask; Arda Wiraz Namag; Bundahisn; Denkard; Menog i Xrad; Dadestan i Denig" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), chapters 1-5" .

#
# The sending of a soul, and what the transmission demands around it
#

pmp:MangIWistaspan a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The drinking of wine and the mang of Wishtasp"@en, "Der Trunk aus Wein und dem Mang des Wischtasp"@de ;
    skos:definition "A procedure for sending a soul out of a living body and getting a report back. The transmission states it as a sequence with numbers. From the whole assembly seven men without doubt are set apart, from the seven three, from the three one; the lot is cast three times, under the names good thought, good word and good deed, and all three fall to the same man. He prays for the departed, eats, makes his testament, washes head and body, puts on new clothes, perfumes himself, has a clean carpet spread on a prepared couch, consecrates the dron and remembers the souls, and eats again. Then three golden cups of wine and the mang of Wishtasp are given him, one spoken over with each of the three names."@en ;
    skos:altLabel "mang i Wishtaspan"@en, "mang"@en, "bang"@en ;
    skos:note "The order in which the preparation is given differs slightly between the verses as numbered and the sequence a reader reconstructs; the testament stands close to the prayer, and the second meal after the consecration. What is fixed and not reconstructed is the count: seven, three, one, three lots, three cups."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 1.28-43 and 2.18-31" .

pmp:WirazWatch a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The seven days' watch over the body"@en, "Die siebentägige Wache über dem Körper"@de ;
    skos:definition "What is done while the traveller is gone. The dasturs give the seven sisters an undertaking in a number: they will deliver him back safe and sound in seven days. The body remains on the carpet; the dasturs, the priests and the seven sisters keep watch around it for seven days and nights, with fire and fumigation, reciting the Avesta and the Zand, going through the Nasks and chanting the Gathas, and they keep watch in the dark."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the part of the procedure that an outside observer could check, and it is stated in a form that could fail: a named man, a named place, a stated number of days, and a promise made to named women that he will be given back alive."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 2.18 and 2.31-33" .

